  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Shabbir Najab Khan vs The State Of Maharashtra on 18 February, 2021 Author: Prakash D. Naik Bench: Prakash D. Naik Ganesh Lokhande 1 of 4 aba In The High Court Of Judicature At Bombay Criminal Appellate Jurisdiction Criminal Anticipatory Bail Application No.1018 Of 2020 Shabbir Najab Khan ...Applicant Versus The State of Maharashtra ...Responden ..... Mr. Amin Solkar, Advocate for the Applicant. Mr. Y. M. Nakhwa, App for the Respondent - State. ..... Coram : Prakash D. Naik, J. Date : 18th February, 2021. Pc : 1.",
    "facts": "The case of the prosecution is that, on 9 th Sep 11:05:25 +0530 Shabbir Najab Khan vs The State Of Maharashtra on 18 February, 2021 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/95486700/ 1 2020 during patrolling duty, the complainant received information that vehicle containing tobacco and gutaka will be passing from Versova police outpost. PSI-Gaikwad and his team went to Versova village road. One Eicher container bearing No. MH-02-ER-8749 was Ganesh Lokhande 2 of 4 aba-1018-2020.doc passing through the road. Police stopped the vehicle and made enquiry with driver and cleaner. They informed that they are carrying consignment of sarees in the container. On inspection, boxes containing sarees were noticed. On further inspection, bags containing gutaka were noticed. After enquiry with a driver about contents of container, he stated that goods were being transported from Surat, Gujarat to Bandra, Mumbai. Police seized the container. FIR was registered. 3. The applicant preferred an application for anticipatory bail before the Court of Sessions, which was rejected by Order dated 9th October, 2020. Driver and cleaner were arrested on 10 th September, 2020. They were produced before the concerned Court for remand. Shabbir Najab Khan vs The State Of Maharashtra on 18 February, 2021 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/95486700/ 2",
    "arguments": "4. Learned counsel for the applicant submitted that the applicant has been falsely implicated in this case. There is nothing to be recovered from the applicant. He had allegedly transported all the articles, seized by the police. The applicant is not named in the FIR. He has been implicated on the basis of statement of co-accused. He was merely the owner of the truck. He has cooperated with the investigation. Learned APP submitted that the applicant's involvement is supported by evidence. The applicant is in the business of transport and he is also owner of the vehicle. The arrested accused were working as a driver. Cleaner on the vehicle had disclosed involvement of the applicant. They have stated that goods were loaded as per directions of the applicant and he knows the source of goods. The applicant is prime accused. His custodial interrogation is necessary. 6. Learned APP relied upon the decision of this Court in the case of Vinod Ramnath Gupta V/s. State of Maharashtra dated 6th November, 2020 and submitted that this Court has held that Section 328 of IPC is attracted in such cases and application was rejected. He also pointed out that investigation papers and the documents which shows that the vehicle used in the crime is in the name of applicant.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "4. Learned counsel for the applicant submitted that the applicant has been falsely implicated in this case. There is nothing to be recovered from the applicant. He had allegedly transported all the articles, seized by the police. The applicant is not named in the FIR. He has been implicated on the basis of statement of co-accused. He was merely the owner of the truck. He has cooperated with the investigation. Learned APP submitted that the applicant's involvement is supported by evidence. The applicant is in the business of transport and he is also owner of the vehicle. The arrested accused were working as a driver. Cleaner on the vehicle had disclosed involvement of the applicant. They have stated that goods were loaded as per directions of the applicant and he knows the source of goods. The applicant is prime accused. His custodial interrogation is necessary. 6. Learned APP relied upon the decision of this Court in the case of Vinod Ramnath Gupta V/s. State of Maharashtra dated 6th November, 2020 and submitted that this Court has held that Section 328 of IPC is attracted in such cases and application was rejected.",
    "respondent_arguments": "He also pointed out that investigation papers and the documents which shows that the vehicle used in the crime is in the name of applicant.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
